        <!-- ============ Page 6: FERS overview (FERS only) ============ -->
        <apex:outputPanel layout="none" rendered="{!Lead.FERS__c == true}">
        <table class="gfgp-sheet">
            <tr>
                <td class="gfgp-body">
                    <div class="gfgp-title">FERS - Federal Employee Retirement System</div>
                    <div class="gfgp-accent"></div>

                    <p class="gfgp-p">
                        The Federal Employees Retirement System, or FERS, became effective January 1, 1987.
                        Almost all new employees hired after December 31, 1983, are automatically covered by
                        FERS. Certain other Federal employees not covered by FERS have the option to transfer
                        into the plan. The FERS retirement system is composed of three tiers:
                    </p>
                    <ol class="gfgp-ol">
                        <li>The Basic Benefit Plan - FERS Retirement Annuity</li>
                        <li>Social Security</li>
                        <li>Thrift Savings Plan</li>
                    </ol>

                    <div class="gfgp-h2">Basic Benefit Plan - FERS Retirement Annuity</div>
                    <ul class="gfgp-ul">
                        <li><b>Eligibility</b> - If you were automatically covered by FERS, or you elected to
                            transfer from CSRS, you will participate in the Basic Benefit plan. To be vested and
                            receive retirement benefits from this plan, you must have at least 5 years of creditable
                            civilian service. Survivor and disability benefits are available after 18 months of service.</li>
                        <li><b>Contributions by employees into FERS</b></li>
                    </ul>
                    <ol class="gfgp-ol">
                        <li>FERS - Hired on/after 1/1/1987 - Code K - Contribution 0.8%</li>
                        <li>FERS-RAE - Hired on/after 1/1/2013 - Code KR - Contribution 3.1%</li>
                        <li>FERS-FRAE - Hired on/after 1/1/2014 - Code KF - Contribution 4.4%</li>
                        <li>Special Groups contributions are always .5% higher</li>
                    </ol>
                    <ul class="gfgp-ul">
                        <li><b>Refunds</b> - You may withdraw your basic benefit contributions, if you leave Federal
                            employment. However, if you do, you will not be eligible to receive benefits based on
                            service covered by the refund. Rules regarding redeposits of refunded FERS contributions
                            can vary based on service dates and individual circumstances. Employees should consult
                            OPM or a qualified retirement specialist regarding redeposit eligibility.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <p class="gfgp-p">Retirement Options - There are three categories of retirement benefits in the Basic Benefit Plan:</p>
                    <p class="gfgp-p" style="margin-left:14pt;"><b>1. Immediate or Postponed:</b> If you meet one of the following sets of age and service requirements, you are entitled to an immediate retirement benefit:</p>
                    <ul class="gfgp-ul gfgp-bold-li">
                        <li>Age 62 with at least 5 years of creditable service (unreduced benefit)</li>
                        <li>Age 60 with at least 20 years of creditable service (unreduced benefit)</li>
                        <li>MRA 30 (Minimum Retirement Age) with at least 30 years of service. <span style="font-weight:normal;">See chart (pg. 8)</span></li>
                        <li>MRA 10 (Minimum Retirement Age) with at least 10 years of service. <span style="font-weight:normal;">See note below</span></li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="gfgp-p">
                        <span style="color:#B91C1C;">Note: MRA 10 - If you retire under MRA 10 (at least 10 years of service but less than 30 years) <b>your FERS Annuity will be reduced by 5% for every year you are under the age of 62.</b></span>
                        Unless you have 20 years of service and your annuity begins at age 60 or later, you can avoid part, or all of the reduction by postponing the commencing date of your annuity.
                    </p>
                    <p class="gfgp-p" style="margin-left:14pt;"><b>2. Early Retirement</b> - An early retirement benefit is available in certain involuntary separation cases and in cases of voluntary separations, during a major reorganization, or reduction in force. To be eligible, you must meet the following requirements:</p>
                    <ul class="gfgp-ul gfgp-bold-li">
                        <li>Age 50 with 20 years of service</li>
                        <li>Any age with 25 years of service</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="gfgp-p" style="margin-left:14pt;"><b>3. Deferred Retirement</b> - If you leave Federal service before you meet the age and service requirements for an immediate retirement benefit, you may be eligible for deferred retirement benefits. To be eligible, you must have completed at least 5 years of creditable civilian service. You may receive benefits when you meet one of the following sets of age and service requirements:</p>
                    <ul class="gfgp-ul gfgp-bold-li">
                        <li>Age 62 with 5 years of service &#8226; Age 60 with 20 years of service &#8226; MRA 30</li>
                        <li>MRA 10 (Reduced benefit unless receipt is delayed to lessen or avoid age reduction)</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td class="gfgp-rail" valign="bottom"><div class="gfgp-pageno">6</div></td>
            </tr>
        </table>
        </apex:outputPanel>

        <!-- ============ Page 7: Calculating FERS Retirement (FERS only) ============ -->
        <apex:outputPanel layout="none" rendered="{!Lead.FERS__c == true}">
        <table class="gfgp-sheet">
            <tr>
                <td class="gfgp-rail" valign="bottom"><div class="gfgp-pageno">7</div></td>
                <td class="gfgp-body">
                    <div class="gfgp-title">FERS - Calculating FERS Retirement</div>
                    <div class="gfgp-accent"></div>
                    <p class="gfgp-p"><b>Benefit Formula</b> - How your benefit is calculated: Your benefit is based on your "high-three average pay." This is figured by averaging your highest basic pay over any 3 consecutive years of creditable service. In addition to your high-three, your years of creditable service and age at retirement are key factors. Calculating your FERS Annuity:</p>
                    <ul class="gfgp-red-ul">
                        <li>If under age 62, your factor is 1% X High-Three X Years of Service</li>
                        <li>At age 62 or older (20 plus years of service), your factor is 1.1% X High-Three X Years of Service</li>
                    </ul>

                    <!-- Eligibility / Minimum Retirement Age table -->
                    <table class="gfgp-elig">
                        <thead>
                            <tr>
                                <th>If your year of birth is...</th>
                                <th>Your minimum retirement age is...</th>
                                <th>Retirement</th>
